$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The case</h2>
<p>The petitioner was arrested in January 2025 in a case registered at Police Station Bichhwal, District Bikaner, on the allegation of recovery of 102 grams of MDMA — above the commercial-quantity threshold — with offences under Sections 8 and 22 of the NDPS Act. By the time the third bail application was heard before Justice Mukesh Rajpurohit, the chargesheet had been filed and one of sixteen prosecution witnesses had been examined. An earlier application had been dismissed as not pressed, with liberty to renew after the seizure officer's evidence.</p>

<h2>The grounds pressed, and why they failed</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Procedural objections</strong> — the defence argued non-compliance with Section 50, an unexplained gap of about forty days between recovery and the samples reaching the FSL, and questions over the seizure officer's authority. The Court left all of these open for trial, declining to treat them as grounds that displace Section 37 at the bail stage.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Clean antecedents and filed challan</strong> — relevant in an ordinary case, but not sufficient to satisfy the statutory requirement of reasonable grounds to believe the accused is not guilty.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Custody and trial pace</strong> — over twenty months in custody with the trial barely begun. The Court, relying on recent Supreme Court rulings, held that delay does not by itself override the embargo.</div>
</div>

<h2>The statutory logic</h2>
<p>Section 37 inverts the ordinary presumption in favour of bail. For commercial-quantity cases, the legislature has made pre-trial liberty conditional on a prima facie judicial assessment of innocence — a deliberately high bar. The Court recorded in terms that neither of the twin conditions stood fulfilled: on the material before it, there were no reasonable grounds to believe the petitioner was not guilty, nor that he would not offend on bail. Once that finding is made, the usual bail considerations never come into play.</p>

<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>Ordinary bail logic</h3><p>Bail is the rule; custody must be justified by flight risk, tampering, or gravity. Delay in trial strengthens the plea for liberty.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>Section 37 logic</h3><p>Custody is the statutory default for commercial quantity; liberty must be justified by a prima facie view of innocence. Delay alone does not open the gate.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>For the defence in commercial-quantity NDPS cases:</strong> build the Section 37 argument on the merits of the recovery itself — independent witnesses, link evidence, sampling integrity, quantity disputes — rather than on custody arithmetic; preserve every procedural objection in cross-examination so it matures into a trial defence; and when renewing bail, come with a genuine change in circumstances, such as material elicited in evidence, not a restatement of the last application.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>The decision does not say that NDPS undertrials are without remedy — it says the remedy must fit the statute. Where the recovery is of commercial quantity, the realistic paths are a trial fought on the safeguards the Act itself mandates, or custody of such extraordinary length that constitutional principles intervene. Neither is engaged by repetition of grounds the statute has already answered.</p>
</div>
